WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.020
You know, it happens all the time, actually.

00:03.140 --> 00:04.960
You check your phone on a Sunday afternoon, right?

00:05.020 --> 00:06.320
And you pull up a sports app.

00:06.500 --> 00:07.500
Yeah, just checking the scores.

00:07.800 --> 00:08.020
Right.

00:08.120 --> 00:13.720
You look at a basketball scoreboard, and it says the home team won 104-98.

00:14.240 --> 00:15.200
Pretty close game.

00:15.360 --> 00:15.800
Exactly.

00:16.200 --> 00:20.000
And as you look at those numbers, you know precisely who won.

00:20.360 --> 00:27.020
I mean, it is a completely accurate, totally factual record of the final state of that game.

00:27.080 --> 00:27.640
It's the truth.

00:27.940 --> 00:28.600
It is the truth.

00:28.600 --> 00:36.740
But looking only at that final score, you have absolutely no idea how those 48 minutes actually unfolded.

00:36.880 --> 00:37.240
None at all.

00:37.420 --> 00:39.580
Like, was it a complete blowout early on?

00:39.900 --> 00:46.840
Did the winning team, you know, just rest their starters in the fourth quarter and allow some late, totally meaningless rally?

00:47.020 --> 00:50.420
Or was it a neck-and-neck buzzer beater, right, where the lead changed 20 times?

00:50.560 --> 00:51.040
Exactly.

00:51.560 --> 00:54.140
The numbers on your screen are 100% honest.

00:54.280 --> 00:55.680
I mean, they aren't lying to you at all.

00:55.680 --> 00:59.680
But they have completely, utterly stripped away the narrative.

01:00.100 --> 01:01.460
And we see this in other places, too.

01:01.720 --> 01:04.080
Think about looking at a digital map of airline traffic.

01:04.200 --> 01:05.620
Oh, yeah, like the little flight trackers.

01:05.920 --> 01:06.200
Right.

01:06.640 --> 01:12.360
You see this tiny little green dot land at Heathrow Airport after taking off from JFK in New York.

01:12.480 --> 01:14.740
So you know exactly where those passengers arrived.

01:14.740 --> 01:22.940
Yes, but that single dot on a map tells you literally nothing about the turbulent weather they flew through over the Atlantic.

01:23.300 --> 01:24.940
Or the holding patterns they had to endure.

01:25.080 --> 01:25.500
Exactly.

01:25.940 --> 01:32.300
Or, you know, the life-changing conversation two strangers might have had in row 12B while they were suspended in transit.

01:32.540 --> 01:37.920
The destination is preserved perfectly, but the journey, the actual journey, has been entirely erased.

01:37.920 --> 01:39.180
And we accept that erasure.

01:39.460 --> 01:43.140
We accept it because in those moments, we only care about the destination.

01:43.720 --> 01:46.840
We only ask the app who won or where the plane landed.

01:47.040 --> 01:47.300
Right.

01:47.400 --> 01:56.320
And that concept that a record can be perfectly mathematically honest while simultaneously being fundamentally incomplete, that is the entire foundation of our mission today.

01:56.740 --> 01:58.520
It's a huge concept to wrap your head around.

01:58.520 --> 01:59.220
It really is.

01:59.220 --> 02:07.820
We are taking a deep dive today into a really fascinating companion book by independent researcher Flyxion titled, What Probability Forgets?

02:08.300 --> 02:11.940
Projection, Interference, and the Hidden Architecture of Quantum Theory.

02:12.260 --> 02:14.140
A heavy title, but it's brilliant.

02:14.300 --> 02:15.060
It's so brilliant.

02:15.340 --> 02:20.100
And the mission we have for you, the listener, today is pretty ambitious, honestly.

02:20.300 --> 02:24.260
We want to fundamentally change how you view quantum mechanics.

02:24.260 --> 02:33.760
Because, let's be honest, most of the time when quantum physics enters the conversation, it is treated like this mystical catalog of oddities.

02:33.900 --> 02:34.380
Oh, totally.

02:34.780 --> 02:38.980
It's always cats that are simultaneously alive and dead inside locked boxes, right?

02:39.100 --> 02:39.320
Yeah.

02:39.520 --> 02:42.660
Or particles that can magically communicate faster than light.

02:42.840 --> 02:47.680
Or the universe constantly splitting into infinite multiverses every single time you make a decision.

02:47.840 --> 02:50.060
Which, you know, makes for incredible science fiction.

02:50.220 --> 02:50.660
It does.

02:50.740 --> 02:51.580
It's great for movies.

02:51.580 --> 02:59.100
But it often completely obscures the actual, grounded, logical machinery operating beneath the surface.

02:59.360 --> 03:02.300
I mean, it treats quantum mechanics as a departure from logic.

03:02.480 --> 03:02.680
Right.

03:02.740 --> 03:04.520
Rather than a deeper application of it.

03:04.640 --> 03:05.100
Precisely.

03:05.200 --> 03:15.260
So instead of focusing on the spooky, magical stuff, you know, we are going to explore quantum mechanics as a disciplined, precise mathematical architecture.

03:15.500 --> 03:20.280
We're looking at it as a framework designed to solve one specific practical problem.

03:20.280 --> 03:26.340
Which is how to preserve the vital information that ordinary probability intentionally leaves behind.

03:26.500 --> 03:34.340
And when you look at quantum theory through this specific lens, the supposed magic, it just starts to vanish.

03:34.500 --> 03:35.080
It really does.

03:35.140 --> 03:35.860
It just falls away.

03:36.000 --> 03:38.300
And something much more elegant takes its place.

03:39.160 --> 03:44.540
We're going to see that the famous concept of quantum interference isn't mystical at all.

03:44.540 --> 03:52.520
Because it's usually explained to us as, like, a particle somehow knowing about alternative paths it didn't even take.

03:52.660 --> 03:52.700
Right.

03:52.700 --> 03:53.140
Exactly.

03:53.400 --> 03:54.840
Which sounds like magic.

03:55.360 --> 03:56.380
But it's not.

03:56.800 --> 03:58.360
It is simply returning memory.

03:58.680 --> 04:02.720
It is the math recovering information that a probability table threw away.

04:02.880 --> 04:03.720
Returning memory.

04:03.920 --> 04:05.140
That phrase is so good.

04:05.220 --> 04:07.880
That idea is where we really need to anchor ourselves today.

04:07.880 --> 04:12.660
But to understand how memory returns, I guess we have to understand how it gets lost in the first place.

04:12.960 --> 04:14.020
The loss is the first step.

04:14.140 --> 04:17.180
So the source text starts with this concept called the honest shadow.

04:17.520 --> 04:23.660
Flyxion asks us to imagine a probability not as the whole truth of a system, but as a shadow cast on a wall.

04:23.820 --> 04:24.900
Which is a great visualization.

04:25.520 --> 04:25.680
Yeah.

04:25.780 --> 04:27.920
Let's build a mental model for you right now.

04:28.420 --> 04:32.620
Imagine you have a mysterious sealed metal box on a table.

04:32.760 --> 04:32.980
Okay.

04:33.040 --> 04:33.780
A black box.

04:33.780 --> 04:34.040
Right.

04:34.340 --> 04:39.220
You feed a blank piece of paper into a slot on this box, and on top of the box are two lamps.

04:39.720 --> 04:40.820
Lamp A and lamp B.

04:41.020 --> 04:41.640
Simple enough.

04:41.820 --> 04:44.560
And you run this machine, let's say, a thousand times.

04:44.820 --> 04:48.280
You sit there with a clipboard, and you build a table of the results.

04:48.720 --> 04:49.500
Lots of data.

04:49.760 --> 04:52.460
50% of the time, lamp A lights up.

04:52.680 --> 04:54.820
50% of the time, lamp B lights up.

04:54.900 --> 04:55.100
Okay.

04:55.760 --> 05:03.400
That probability table, that 50-50 split, is a totally truthful, operationally meaningful record of what the machine produces.

05:03.400 --> 05:05.020
It acts as a perfect witness.

05:05.200 --> 05:05.900
A witness, right.

05:06.020 --> 05:10.240
The text makes this crucial distinction here between a witness and a mechanism.

05:10.480 --> 05:10.680
Okay.

05:10.840 --> 05:11.700
Unpack that for me.

05:11.760 --> 05:12.460
What's the difference?

05:12.700 --> 05:14.400
So, a probability table is a witness.

05:15.080 --> 05:21.360
It faithfully records that some transformation happened, and it summarizes the visible consequences for you.

05:21.380 --> 05:22.100
Like the scoreboard.

05:22.400 --> 05:23.660
Exactly like the scoreboard.

05:23.920 --> 05:26.600
But a mechanism is something structurally deeper.

05:27.240 --> 05:29.440
A mechanism supports counterfactuals.

05:29.500 --> 05:31.080
Meaning what could have happened.

05:31.600 --> 05:31.840
Right.

05:31.940 --> 05:33.720
It explains how things connect.

05:34.140 --> 05:36.160
How transformations combine internally.

05:37.060 --> 05:49.440
And critically, a mechanism distinguishes between two entirely different internal physical processes that might just happen to cast the exact same 50-50 probabilistic shadow on your wall.

05:49.540 --> 05:50.080
Okay, hold on.

05:50.200 --> 05:51.980
Let me challenge the premise of this for a moment.

05:52.060 --> 05:52.680
Sure, go for it.

05:52.680 --> 05:59.940
Because if I take a standard quarter out of my pocket right now and flip it, the probability of it landing on heads is 50%.

05:59.940 --> 06:02.160
And tails is 50%.

06:02.160 --> 06:03.680
Classic probability.

06:03.900 --> 06:04.100
Right.

06:04.340 --> 06:09.740
And the standard way we think about the world is that the 50-50 split is the whole story of a coin flip.

06:09.740 --> 06:12.720
It's a complete record of the randomness.

06:13.080 --> 06:13.760
It's how we're taught it.

06:14.280 --> 06:17.120
So, why do we need to assume there is a hidden mechanism?

06:17.120 --> 06:21.720
How can a perfect probability record of a coin flip be missing anything?

06:22.420 --> 06:27.200
Well, physically, it is missing the specific rotational dynamics of the coin.

06:27.620 --> 06:30.340
You know, the air resistance, the force of your thumb.

06:30.400 --> 06:31.540
Okay, yeah, the physics of it.

06:31.840 --> 06:44.140
But more importantly, from a mathematical standpoint, a probability table is only considered a complete story because you are only asking it one specific isolated question.

06:44.340 --> 06:45.920
One isolated question.

06:45.920 --> 06:51.420
Right. In statistics, there is a concept that predates quantum mechanics by decades.

06:51.880 --> 06:54.420
It's called task-relative sufficiency.

06:54.920 --> 06:57.580
Task-relative sufficiency. Okay, what does that mean?

06:57.800 --> 07:05.440
A sufficient statistic is a summary of data that loses absolutely nothing relevant to one particular question you have chosen to ask.

07:05.540 --> 07:08.500
The book uses a really intuitive analogy for this, actually.

07:08.740 --> 07:10.120
Taking an average...

07:10.120 --> 07:11.600
Oh, yes, the temperature analogy.

07:11.600 --> 07:17.340
Yeah. So, suppose you take a digital thermometer and you measure the temperature in a room 10 different times over an hour.

07:17.560 --> 07:18.760
You'd get slightly different reading.

07:18.840 --> 07:24.180
Exactly. You get a bunch of slightly different numbers. 71.2 degrees, 72.5, 71.8, and so on.

07:24.600 --> 07:25.000
Fluctuations.

07:25.160 --> 07:28.840
Right. And you average them all out and you tell me, hey, it's 72 degrees in here.

07:28.980 --> 07:29.520
Which is helpful.

07:29.520 --> 07:35.880
It is. But by giving me that single average, you have literally deleted the raw data.

07:36.200 --> 07:38.540
You threw away every single individual reading.

07:38.700 --> 07:39.860
You erased the history.

07:40.120 --> 07:42.180
But I don't accuse you of lying to me.

07:42.740 --> 07:46.520
We don't view the concept of an average as some kind of mathematical scandal.

07:47.280 --> 07:53.840
Right. Because the average is a sufficient statistic for the only question I care to ask, which was, should I put on a sweater?

07:53.840 --> 08:02.420
Exactly. You willingly accepted the destruction of the individual variations because of the specific task you were performing.

08:02.740 --> 08:04.380
And that's exactly what's happened with the coin flip.

08:04.880 --> 08:12.800
The probability table of your coin flip, or that 50-50 lamp machine, is task relatively sufficient.

08:13.260 --> 08:15.260
So it's flawless for that one task.

08:15.260 --> 08:26.620
It is a flawless description if, and only if, your only question is, what happens if I use this machine exactly once, in total isolation, and I never connect it to any other process?

08:26.780 --> 08:28.520
And that is the hinge point, isn't it?

08:28.640 --> 08:29.320
It really is.

08:29.580 --> 08:37.880
Probability is a fantastic tool for isolated events, but the trouble, according to the text, begins the absolute second you try to chain events together.

08:38.020 --> 08:39.340
The moment you try to compose them.

08:39.340 --> 08:45.400
Right. So let's say you take that 50-50 lamp machine we talked about, and you build a second identical lamp machine.

08:45.540 --> 08:46.960
Okay. Machine 1 and Machine 2.

08:47.300 --> 08:53.840
And you take the output of Machine 1, whatever it is, and you plug it directly into the input of Machine 2.

08:53.980 --> 08:54.920
So we're chaining them.

08:55.100 --> 08:55.920
Chaining them together.

08:56.380 --> 09:01.680
Now, common sense and classical probability theory tells us exactly how to handle this.

09:01.760 --> 09:02.680
You just multiply them.

09:02.680 --> 09:13.040
Right. If Machine 1 connects to Machine 2, and they are both perfect 50-50 randomizers, I should just multiply their probability tables together, half times half.

09:13.160 --> 09:14.500
That's what standard math would say.

09:14.560 --> 09:20.760
So the output of the second machine should just be a flattened out, totally randomized mess of probabilities.

09:20.760 --> 09:26.500
Like shuffling a deck of cards thoroughly, handing it to a friend, and having them shuffle it thoroughly again.

09:26.600 --> 09:28.800
Exactly. The deck just stays completely randomized.

09:29.060 --> 09:31.800
That is what the probability shadow predicts will happen.

09:31.800 --> 09:36.200
And that is exactly where classical probability fails to compose.

09:36.480 --> 09:36.880
It fails.

09:37.000 --> 09:37.840
It completely fails.

09:38.060 --> 09:43.280
When mathematicians say compose, they just mean linking operations together in a sequence.

09:43.440 --> 09:44.200
One after the other.

09:44.620 --> 09:44.860
Right.

09:45.260 --> 09:58.040
If you compress a physical process down into a flat probability shadow, and then you try to multiply those shadows together to predict a sequence of events, you do not always get the correct prediction for the final outcome.

09:58.320 --> 10:01.540
Flyxion makes this explicit in Chapter 8, right?

10:01.800 --> 10:06.920
There's a mathematical example that is basically the engine for this entire framework.

10:07.160 --> 10:07.480
Yes.

10:07.780 --> 10:12.560
Let's walk through that example carefully because it fundamentally alters how you view randomness.

10:12.560 --> 10:13.200
Okay, let's do it.

10:13.200 --> 10:16.040
So the author sets up two mathematical operations.

10:16.560 --> 10:18.580
We will call them U1 and U2.

10:19.080 --> 10:22.880
Think of them as two different physical machines placed in a sequence on a factory line.

10:22.880 --> 10:29.440
Okay, so if you look at machine U1 strictly through the lens of probability, it behaves exactly like our coin flip.

10:29.600 --> 10:30.900
A perfect 50-50 split.

10:31.000 --> 10:31.160
Right.

10:31.380 --> 10:38.520
You put a signal in, and a probability table says you have a 50% chance of getting output option one and a 50% chance of getting output option two.

10:38.700 --> 10:40.380
It is a perfect randomizer.

10:40.380 --> 10:42.580
And then you look at machine U2 in isolation.

10:42.800 --> 10:43.000
Right.

10:43.160 --> 10:43.960
Exact same story.

10:44.100 --> 10:46.760
It takes whatever you give it and randomizes it 50-50.

10:47.120 --> 10:54.740
Okay, so naturally, if you run a signal through U1 and feed that randomized 50-50 output directly into U2.

10:55.160 --> 10:58.600
The resulting probability table should still be completely uniform.

10:59.080 --> 11:03.380
A 50-50 spread multiplied by a 50-50 spread equals uniform randomness.

11:03.380 --> 11:06.720
But that is not what actually happens in the quantum architecture.

11:06.860 --> 11:07.040
What's there?

11:07.120 --> 11:07.860
It gets crazy.

11:07.980 --> 11:13.180
Behind those slat 50-50 probability shadows, there are underlying structures called amplitudes.

11:13.380 --> 11:13.840
Amplitudes.

11:14.260 --> 11:14.440
Yes.

11:14.760 --> 11:16.380
And amplitudes contain something called phase.

11:17.080 --> 11:17.780
Phase, okay.

11:17.860 --> 11:29.460
When you compose the actual amplitude matrices of U1 and U2 together, meaning you multiply the full mechanisms, not just their shadows, the result defies classical logic.

11:29.580 --> 11:31.540
All of the uncertainty completely vanishes.

11:31.680 --> 11:32.600
It vanishes entirely.

11:32.600 --> 11:33.820
That's wild.

11:34.180 --> 11:42.780
When you compose the full amplitudes of U2 and U1, the cross terms, which are the hidden relational data points between the pathways, they interact.

11:42.980 --> 11:44.260
And depending on their phase.

11:44.340 --> 11:44.780
Exactly.

11:44.980 --> 11:48.800
Depending on their phase, they either reinforce each other or cancel each other out perfectly.

11:49.080 --> 11:56.240
So the final result, after your signal passes through two seemingly perfect randomizers, is 100% certainty.

11:56.540 --> 11:58.980
The original input returns exactly as it was.

11:59.340 --> 12:01.660
The system's identity is perfectly restored.

12:01.660 --> 12:07.600
The source text has an analoger for this that is incredibly clarifying, I think.

12:07.720 --> 12:08.980
Oh, the cipher analogy.

12:09.160 --> 12:09.420
Yes.

12:09.820 --> 12:12.160
Imagine I want to send you a secret text message.

12:12.540 --> 12:16.200
I encrypt the message using a complex, reversible cipher.

12:16.200 --> 12:17.320
So it looks like nonsense.

12:17.520 --> 12:17.720
Right?

12:18.300 --> 12:26.900
But instead of sending you the encrypted gibberish text, I run a statistical analysis on it, and I only hand you a table showing the frequency of the letters used.

12:27.020 --> 12:29.480
Like how many times the letter E appears.

12:29.480 --> 12:30.040
Exactly.

12:30.280 --> 12:36.560
If you look at a letter frequency table of an encrypted message, it just looks like a uniform 50-50 soup of data.

12:36.740 --> 12:38.060
It looks like random noise.

12:38.200 --> 12:39.620
Because you threw away the sequence.

12:39.840 --> 12:40.080
Right.

12:40.300 --> 12:44.840
If you try to apply the second half of the cipher key to that frequency table, nothing happens.

12:44.940 --> 12:46.060
You just get more noise.

12:46.200 --> 12:47.600
Because the structure is gone.

12:47.600 --> 13:01.400
But if you have the next piece of the cipher and you apply it to the underlying encrypted text, the actual hidden relational data, not the frequency table, the original text message returns perfectly.

13:01.720 --> 13:06.720
This completely deconstructs the traditional mystical view of quantum interference.

13:07.020 --> 13:07.700
It really does.

13:08.100 --> 13:12.420
Because you usually hear interference explained using the famous double-slid experiment.

13:12.660 --> 13:16.960
Yeah, where a single photon approaches two microscopic slits in a barrier.

13:16.960 --> 13:25.820
And somehow the photon knows about the path it didn't take, or it splits in half and goes through both, or the universe branches into two realities.

13:25.920 --> 13:32.100
We invent these massive metaphysical narratives to explain how an interference pattern appears on the wall behind the slits.

13:32.340 --> 13:36.040
But Flixiana's approach strips all of that heavy metaphysics away.

13:36.220 --> 13:36.680
Completely.

13:37.380 --> 13:40.700
Interference isn't the probability table magically changing its mind.

13:40.860 --> 13:41.140
Right.

13:41.140 --> 13:45.060
It isn't a particle possessing clairvoyance about the laboratory setup.

13:45.060 --> 13:53.040
Interference is simply the mathematical proof that the initial probability table you were looking at was never the whole state of the process to begin with.

13:53.220 --> 13:58.180
The initial probability table was an honest shadow, but it was structurally incomplete.

13:58.580 --> 13:59.800
It was missing the phase.

14:00.120 --> 14:07.620
The relational information, the phase, the orientation, the memory of how the different internal parts of the system relate to one another.

14:07.620 --> 14:12.500
It was intentionally thrown away the moment you projected it down into a flat probability.

14:13.060 --> 14:22.640
And when you compose the system, you know, when you let it continue its journey and interact with the next step, that forgotten relational information comes back to affect the outcome.

14:22.640 --> 14:30.560
So, quantum phase isn't some tiny physical ocean wave literally sloshing around inside a microscopic particle.

14:30.700 --> 14:31.560
No, not at all.

14:31.680 --> 14:33.440
Phase is compositional memory.

14:34.160 --> 14:40.680
It is the mathematical bookkeeping required to make sure that when two transformations combine, they don't contradict each other's history.

14:40.960 --> 14:43.260
It's just making sure the math stays honest over time.

14:43.260 --> 14:47.900
But combining two machines in a straight, linear sequence is just moving forward.

14:48.400 --> 14:51.040
The text points out that straight lines are relatively easy.

14:51.240 --> 14:52.420
Linear is always easier.

14:52.480 --> 14:52.700
Right.

14:53.000 --> 14:57.260
The real structural crisis happens when we try to connect multiple perspectives in a loop.

14:57.360 --> 14:57.960
Oh, the cycles.

14:58.420 --> 15:02.040
Yeah, when things have to agree with each other from all sides simultaneously.

15:02.600 --> 15:10.680
This brings us to what the text calls context graphs and the fundamental architectural difference between trees and cycles.

15:10.980 --> 15:12.040
Okay, let's break this down.

15:12.040 --> 15:16.980
Imagine mapping out different measurement contexts as points on a whiteboard.

15:17.700 --> 15:18.460
Like little dots.

15:18.640 --> 15:18.760
Right.

15:19.020 --> 15:26.420
You draw lines or edges between these points whenever you can define a valid, logical transition from one context to the next.

15:26.620 --> 15:30.740
And a tree, in graph theory, is a structure that just keeps branching outward.

15:30.980 --> 15:32.420
It never loops back on itself.

15:32.520 --> 15:34.960
It looks like a family tree or the branches of an oak.

15:35.020 --> 15:35.380
Exactly.

15:35.860 --> 15:40.320
And on a tree structure, local admissibility is all you ever need.

15:40.320 --> 15:42.560
What does that mean, local admissibility?

15:42.980 --> 15:54.600
It means if you start at the trunk or the root frame and move outward step by step along the branches, assigning your probabilities and your amplitudes as you go, you will never run into a logical contradiction.

15:54.920 --> 15:55.220
Never.

15:55.520 --> 15:55.820
Never.

15:56.040 --> 15:56.760
The reason is simple.

15:56.760 --> 16:00.480
You are constantly stepping into fresh, untouched territory.

16:01.300 --> 16:04.920
Every edge you cross is a new question being asked for the very first time.

16:05.280 --> 16:07.380
There is no prior history to contradict.

16:07.920 --> 16:08.520
Ah, okay.

16:08.700 --> 16:09.420
That makes sense.

16:09.580 --> 16:11.180
Because you're always moving to a new point.

16:11.180 --> 16:14.520
But a cycle is entirely different.

16:14.920 --> 16:17.980
A cycle is a path that eventually loops back on itself.

16:18.100 --> 16:23.280
Meaning you eventually arrive back at a measurement context that you have already defined earlier in your journey.

16:23.420 --> 16:23.640
Yes.

16:24.120 --> 16:28.100
And closing a cycle changes the mathematical burden entirely.

16:28.220 --> 16:28.880
It gets harder.

16:29.080 --> 16:29.520
Much harder.

16:30.300 --> 16:38.920
Cycles convert the problem of continuation, which is just figuring out what happens next, into a much more rigid problem of consistency.

16:39.360 --> 16:39.840
Consistency.

16:40.000 --> 16:40.180
Right.

16:40.500 --> 16:49.500
Does the mathematical answer I get by walking all the way around the left side of the loop perfectly match the answer I already established when I walked around the right side of the loop?

16:49.500 --> 16:56.000
So the text introduces a specific geometric impossibility to demonstrate this burden of consistency.

16:56.520 --> 16:58.180
It's called the triangle obstruction.

16:58.560 --> 17:00.140
This is a beautiful piece of geometry.

17:00.360 --> 17:01.020
It really is.

17:01.400 --> 17:05.600
We have three measurement contexts, which we'll call contexts Z, X, and Y.

17:05.740 --> 17:08.580
And they form a closed loop of three, a triangle.

17:08.580 --> 17:08.900
Right.

17:09.100 --> 17:14.520
And the critical detail here is that these three contexts are mutually unbiased bases.

17:14.920 --> 17:16.380
Okay, let me translate that for you listening.

17:17.380 --> 17:22.780
Mutually unbiased simply means they are as misaligned from each other as mathematically possible.

17:23.060 --> 17:24.040
Maximum uncertainty.

17:24.460 --> 17:24.760
Right.

17:24.940 --> 17:31.460
If you are completely certain about the state of the system in context Z, you are forced to be maximally uncertain.

17:31.660 --> 17:34.720
Exactly 50-50 about its outcome in context X.

17:34.720 --> 17:44.220
Let's try to visualize building this triangle on a flat two-dimensional piece of paper using only ordinary real numbers, just regular geometry.

17:44.420 --> 17:44.980
Let's do it.

17:45.140 --> 17:47.780
Think of a compass or an XY coordinate plane.

17:48.200 --> 17:51.300
You start by drawing line Z pointing straight up at north.

17:51.420 --> 17:52.520
Okay, that's your first context.

17:52.520 --> 18:03.840
Now you need to draw line X because X must be mutually unbiased to Z, meaning 50-50 probability overlap geometry dictates you must draw line X at exactly a 45-degree angle from Z.

18:04.200 --> 18:05.480
So X is pointing northeast.

18:05.720 --> 18:06.120
Exactly.

18:06.320 --> 18:07.040
So far, no problem.

18:07.160 --> 18:08.340
You have two edges of the triangle.

18:08.460 --> 18:09.500
But now the trap closes.

18:09.660 --> 18:10.280
The trap closes.

18:10.480 --> 18:11.560
You have to draw line Y.

18:11.560 --> 18:18.140
The rule state Y must be mutually unbiased to Z, so Y has to be exactly 45 degrees away from Z.

18:18.380 --> 18:18.560
Right.

18:18.840 --> 18:23.620
But because this is a cycle, Y also has to be exactly 45 degrees away from X.

18:23.860 --> 18:25.360
And you immediately hit a wall.

18:25.520 --> 18:31.160
On a flat 2D real number plane, you cannot satisfy all three of those conditions at once.

18:31.220 --> 18:32.500
You physically can't do it.

18:32.500 --> 18:36.640
If Y is 45 degrees away from Z, there are only two places it can go.

18:36.800 --> 18:42.620
It can go northeast, which means it sits exactly on top of X, meaning they aren't unbiased.

18:42.760 --> 18:43.460
They are identical.

18:43.780 --> 18:44.700
Which breaks the rule.

18:44.780 --> 18:44.960
Right.

18:45.200 --> 18:46.800
Or Y can go northwest.

18:47.560 --> 18:53.260
But if Y is pointing northwest and X is pointing northeast, they are now 90 degrees apart from each other.

18:53.400 --> 18:55.540
And at 90 degrees, they are completely disjointed.

18:55.760 --> 18:58.440
The probability overlap is zero, not 50-50.

18:58.580 --> 19:00.500
You literally run out of room on the paper.

19:00.500 --> 19:02.400
The triangle refuses to close.

19:02.500 --> 19:06.180
Any two edges of this scenario work perfectly in isolation.

19:06.780 --> 19:08.140
You can make Z and X work.

19:08.640 --> 19:10.220
You can make X and Y work.

19:10.360 --> 19:19.740
But the moment you demand global consistency across all three at the same time, the flat reality you are trying to construct with real numbers just breaks.

19:20.240 --> 19:23.720
The real-world analogy from the text makes this so tangible, I think.

19:24.100 --> 19:28.120
Imagine a police detective interviewing three witnesses to a complex crime.

19:28.120 --> 19:34.360
Witness A and Witness B tell stories that, when compared side by side, perfectly align with each other.

19:34.540 --> 19:35.140
So they match.

19:35.700 --> 19:38.600
Witness B and Witness C tell stories that perfectly align.

19:39.280 --> 19:42.860
And Witness C and Witness A tell stories that perfectly align.

19:43.060 --> 19:44.460
Pair-wise, they make sense.

19:44.460 --> 19:48.500
Pair-wise, taken two at a time, they make total logical sense.

19:48.500 --> 19:59.000
But when the detective goes to the whiteboard and tries to glue all three stories together onto a single linear timeline, it is structurally impossible.

19:59.320 --> 20:02.500
The events overlap in ways that contradict the laws of physics.

20:02.500 --> 20:03.920
The timeline shatters.

20:04.060 --> 20:10.820
And that breaking point, the geometric shattering, is precisely why quantum theory introduces complex numbers.

20:11.000 --> 20:11.380
Oh.

20:11.680 --> 20:16.340
It is why quantum mechanics relies on the imaginary unit, which mathematicians call I.

20:16.660 --> 20:27.440
This is a huge realization, because usually when people hear about imaginary numbers in quantum physics, they think it means the physics itself is made up, or that particles are interacting with parallel dimensions or something.

20:27.520 --> 20:28.520
Right, they think it's spooky.

20:28.720 --> 20:31.780
But I is actually acting like a mathematical escape hatch.

20:31.780 --> 20:33.240
It is an escape hatch.

20:33.740 --> 20:35.600
It supplies an extra degree of freedom.

20:36.600 --> 20:43.980
By introducing an imaginary axis, you provide a relative phase of rotation that exists outside of the flat, real number plane.

20:44.120 --> 20:45.640
So it pops out of the 2D paper.

20:45.720 --> 20:46.120
Exactly.

20:46.440 --> 20:56.720
That extra-dimensional space allows all three of those stringent, 45-degree, mutually unbiased conditions to be met simultaneously without contradicting one another.

20:56.800 --> 20:57.920
That's incredible.

20:58.360 --> 21:00.680
The complex number isn't there to add mysticism.

21:00.680 --> 21:02.500
It is an architectural necessity.

21:02.500 --> 21:09.020
It enlarges the space of globally compatible arrangements just enough so that the triangle can actually close.

21:09.320 --> 21:17.240
Flyxion makes a point to emphasize that this geometric impossibility isn't just some abstract math puzzle to torture graduate students.

21:17.340 --> 21:17.580
Oh, no.

21:17.640 --> 21:18.300
It's very real.

21:18.300 --> 21:21.640
It has massive, practical, real-world applications today.

21:22.120 --> 21:29.320
This exact geometric obstruction is the core engine of modern quantum cryptography protocols like BB84 and the Six-State Protocol.

21:29.320 --> 21:35.540
Those cryptographic protocols literally exploit this geometric cost of agreement to secure data.

21:35.860 --> 21:37.880
So they use the impossibility of the triangle.

21:38.240 --> 21:46.460
They rely entirely on the fact that information encoded sharply in one context is geometrically forced to be maximally uncertain in another.

21:46.460 --> 21:57.240
So if a hacker tries to eavesdrop using a hidden real number measurement system, they cannot intercept all three contexts without revealing themselves.

21:57.380 --> 21:59.160
Because they can't close the triangle either.

21:59.280 --> 21:59.560
Right.

21:59.940 --> 22:08.840
The act of the eavesdropper trying to force global consistency breaks the geometry, leaving a detectable error rate that alerts the sender and receiver.

22:08.840 --> 22:14.940
The security is guaranteed not by clever software, but by the impossibility of the real number triangle.

22:15.120 --> 22:16.880
It's baked into the math of the universe.

22:17.060 --> 22:19.200
But the text notes a caveat here, right?

22:19.880 --> 22:27.980
In a basic two-outcome system, this mathematical failure of real numbers only shows up when you form a loop, a cycle of three.

22:28.260 --> 22:28.500
Yes.

22:28.660 --> 22:33.720
If you just look at one isolated transition, real numbers and complex numbers look identical.

22:34.220 --> 22:37.280
A scientist wouldn't be able to tell which math the universe was using?

22:37.520 --> 22:38.520
Not locally, no.

22:38.520 --> 22:43.800
But what happens when we look at a system that has three possible outcomes instead of just two?

22:44.180 --> 22:52.060
Ah, that brings us to a phenomenon the text calls the cliff at three dimensions.

22:52.240 --> 22:52.740
The cliff.

22:52.860 --> 23:02.040
As you pointed out, at two outcomes, a single probability shadow could easily have been cast by a real number mechanism or a complex number mechanism.

23:02.380 --> 23:05.980
You have to build a three-part cycle to watch the real numbers fail.

23:06.040 --> 23:06.340
Right.

23:06.340 --> 23:13.780
But once you move up to a system with three possible outcomes, that low-dimensional coincidence completely vanishes.

23:14.060 --> 23:15.220
You step off a cliff.

23:15.320 --> 23:18.080
So at three outcomes, you don't even need to build a cycle anymore.

23:18.220 --> 23:18.880
No, you don't.

23:18.940 --> 23:24.100
A single probability table can prove that real numbers fail and complex numbers are absolutely required.

23:24.200 --> 23:24.420
Yes.

23:24.420 --> 23:29.360
Flyxion illustrates this with the uniform three-by-three discrete Fourier matrix.

23:29.760 --> 23:31.680
Okay, let's break that down because it sounds intimidating.

23:31.900 --> 23:32.560
It's just a grid.

23:32.760 --> 23:32.920
Right.

23:33.020 --> 23:36.100
Imagine a simple three-by-three grid like a tic-tac-toe board.

23:36.500 --> 23:43.580
A uniform discrete Fourier matrix, when squared to give you probability numbers, just gives you a perfectly flat uniform table.

23:43.580 --> 23:44.620
Every square is the same.

23:44.700 --> 23:48.800
Every single square in the tic-tac-toe board has the exact same number in it.

23:49.400 --> 23:50.200
One-third.

23:50.540 --> 23:52.400
One divided by three across the board.

23:52.400 --> 24:00.880
So if you are looking at it purely as a probability shadow, it looks like the most boring, featureless probability table in the world.

24:01.280 --> 24:04.920
It has total uniform randomness across three options.

24:05.280 --> 24:12.140
But if a mathematician tries to reconstruct that specific flat probability table using only real numbers.

24:12.460 --> 24:14.420
Like trying to build a mechanism behind it.

24:14.500 --> 24:14.880
Exactly.

24:14.880 --> 24:22.000
If they try to find a real orthogonal matrix that operates behind that shadow, they find that it's mathematically impossible.

24:22.400 --> 24:22.620
Why?

24:23.120 --> 24:23.920
Why does it fail?

24:24.280 --> 24:36.600
Because to make a three-by-three matrix orthogonal using only real numbers, you are essentially forced to create a grid of plus and minus signs where every row is perfectly balanced and perpendicular to every other row.

24:36.640 --> 24:36.940
Okay.

24:36.940 --> 24:44.760
You are trying to arrange positive and negative vectors in a 3D space such that they all perfectly cancel each other out in specific ways.

24:44.940 --> 24:44.960
Right.

24:45.040 --> 24:45.660
They have to cancel.

24:45.660 --> 24:55.540
And geometry dictates that in three dimensions, a uniform grid of real plus and minus signs with those exact orthogonal properties simply does not exist.

24:55.700 --> 24:56.840
It just doesn't exist.

24:56.980 --> 25:01.200
It's a proven mathematical impossibility related to something called Hadamard matrices.

25:01.200 --> 25:12.180
So, from observing just one featureless probability shadow in a three-outcome system, you can absolutely guarantee that the underlying mechanism cannot be using real numbers.

25:12.560 --> 25:14.700
It must be using the complex plane to operate.

25:15.200 --> 25:15.840
Wow.

25:15.840 --> 25:19.080
So, we've solved a massive piece of the puzzle here.

25:19.980 --> 25:29.980
We've seen how single systems navigate linear sequences and how they navigate closed loops by using the extra rotational space that complex numbers provide.

25:30.100 --> 25:30.340
Yes.

25:30.440 --> 25:31.600
The single systems are sorted.

25:31.780 --> 25:36.020
But what happens when we step outside of a single system entirely?

25:36.440 --> 25:41.980
What happens when two completely separate physical systems try to communicate and interact with each other?

25:41.980 --> 25:47.560
That is where we run headfirst into the problem of joining worlds and the notorious concept of entanglement.

25:47.700 --> 25:47.840
Right.

25:48.340 --> 25:48.740
Entanglement.

25:49.020 --> 25:51.940
Because if we look at this classically, combining systems is trivial.

25:52.140 --> 25:52.780
Oh, it is easy.

25:52.920 --> 26:03.280
If I have a system in my laboratory with certain probabilities and you have a system in your laboratory with your own probabilities and we want to describe them together as one big system, we just use a Cartesian product.

26:03.520 --> 26:04.260
Simple multiplication.

26:04.640 --> 26:06.700
Let's use a restaurant menu as an analogy.

26:06.700 --> 26:16.620
If the appetizer menu has three options and the main course menu has three options, the Cartesian product says there are exactly nine possible meal combinations.

26:16.980 --> 26:18.400
Three times three is nine.

26:18.600 --> 26:19.980
It's incredibly straightforward.

26:20.160 --> 26:25.200
But in quantum theory, you cannot combine systems using a simple Cartesian product.

26:26.080 --> 26:31.740
The architecture requires a much heavier piece of mathematical machinery called a tensor product.

26:31.920 --> 26:33.100
A tensor product.

26:33.100 --> 26:40.880
Yes, and the tensor product does something incredibly profound and counterintuitive to the dimensional space of these systems.

26:41.240 --> 26:44.600
This is the nine versus ten dimension problem from chapter 10.

26:44.960 --> 26:48.620
Let's really unpack this because when I first read it, it felt like an accounting error.

26:48.760 --> 26:53.120
It does feel like a mistake, but it is a brilliant piece of mathematical auditing by the author.

26:53.200 --> 26:54.000
Okay, walk me through it.

26:54.220 --> 26:56.860
Let's say you have two separate real two-level systems.

26:57.400 --> 27:00.520
Think of them as two simple quantum coins, system A and system B.

27:00.720 --> 27:01.020
Okay.

27:01.020 --> 27:06.260
For the sake of the audit, we are restricting ourselves to only using real numbers to see where they fail.

27:06.360 --> 27:07.140
Just to test it.

27:07.340 --> 27:07.560
Right.

27:07.840 --> 27:14.520
If you count up all the observable properties, the independent directions of system A, you find it has three independent parameters.

27:14.880 --> 27:16.980
System B also has three independent parameters.

27:17.240 --> 27:20.640
Okay, so following the restaurant menu logic, three times three is nine.

27:20.640 --> 27:33.220
So if I combine them, local tomography, which is the principle that you can figure out a whole system just by looking at its parts and how they correlate, that tells me there should be nine joint parameters to describe the combined system.

27:33.600 --> 27:34.020
Exactly.

27:34.020 --> 27:44.460
But if you look at the actual mathematical space required to hold a composite of two symmetric real systems, the space doesn't have nine dimensions.

27:45.000 --> 27:45.240
No.

27:45.380 --> 27:46.500
The math demands ten.

27:46.720 --> 27:49.060
There is a missing tenth direction.

27:49.060 --> 27:56.260
I mean, how does a universe consisting of two parts with three parameters each somehow generate ten parameters when they sit next to each other?

27:56.320 --> 27:56.960
Where does it come from?

27:57.200 --> 28:00.720
The text identifies this missing tenth direction as J tensor J.

28:00.880 --> 28:01.720
J tensor J.

28:01.720 --> 28:08.460
It is created by taking an anti-symmetric operator from system A and multiplying it by an anti-symmetric operator from system B.

28:08.820 --> 28:11.280
Let's explain what anti-symmetric means here.

28:12.260 --> 28:19.060
In math, if something is anti-symmetric, it means that if you swap its parts around, it becomes the exact negative of itself.

28:19.400 --> 28:20.460
It flips its sign.

28:20.660 --> 28:20.920
Right.

28:21.140 --> 28:22.140
Now here's the kicker.

28:22.700 --> 28:27.080
An anti-symmetric operator is completely invisible to a local measurement.

28:27.220 --> 28:27.620
Invisible.

28:27.620 --> 28:34.680
If you isolate system A and look at it entirely on its own, this operator vanishes algebraically.

28:34.840 --> 28:35.720
It traces out to Ziri.

28:35.920 --> 28:38.060
It has no physical manifestation locally.

28:38.160 --> 28:38.800
And same for B.

28:38.940 --> 28:39.440
Same for B.

28:39.720 --> 28:45.720
If you isolate system B and look at it entirely on its own, its anti-symmetric operator also vanishes completely.

28:45.920 --> 28:47.780
So neither part can see this property.

28:48.440 --> 28:51.740
But when they join together, their two invisible parts multiply.

28:52.200 --> 28:54.100
A negative times a negative equals a positive.

28:54.300 --> 28:54.580
Yes.

28:54.580 --> 28:59.260
They combine to create a perfectly valid symmetric visible joint property.

28:59.460 --> 28:59.820
Precisely.

29:00.280 --> 29:03.700
This tenth dimension belongs wholly to the relationship between the two systems.

29:04.160 --> 29:08.040
It cannot be reconstructed by taking an inventory of the locally visible parts.

29:08.180 --> 29:08.500
Wow!

29:08.860 --> 29:11.880
It is a property of the whole that simply does not exist in the parts.

29:12.140 --> 29:18.440
And this leads directly into what I consider the most shocking claim in the entire text regarding entanglement.

29:18.440 --> 29:25.380
The author shows us a specific grid of numbers, a mathematical matrix representing a physical state.

29:25.760 --> 29:26.920
The exact same grid.

29:27.220 --> 29:27.480
Right.

29:27.820 --> 29:36.780
If you interpret that exact grid of numbers strictly under the rules of real quantum theory, the math proves that the two systems are intimately entangled.

29:37.260 --> 29:38.140
They are inseparable.

29:38.460 --> 29:40.460
You cannot describe one without describing the other.

29:40.560 --> 29:41.660
That's under the real rules.

29:41.660 --> 29:51.420
But if you take that identical grid of numbers without changing a single digit and you interpret it under the rules of complex quantum theory, it is perfectly separable.

29:51.620 --> 29:52.760
It's just a normal mixture.

29:53.060 --> 29:56.460
It is just an ordinary classical mixture of independent states.

29:56.640 --> 30:00.140
It forces a stunning realization about what entanglement actually is.

30:00.180 --> 30:01.060
It really does.

30:01.060 --> 30:05.220
It shows us that entanglement is not a physical substance painted onto a matrix.

30:05.760 --> 30:10.160
It is in a microscopic glowing tether connecting two particles across space.

30:10.280 --> 30:11.420
Like we always imagine it.

30:11.680 --> 30:11.900
Right.

30:12.960 --> 30:16.220
Entanglement is not intrinsic to a bare grid of numbers.

30:16.980 --> 30:26.240
Entanglement is a property of the fit between a physical state and the background mathematical grammar of the theory you are using to describe it.

30:26.440 --> 30:30.040
I have to use a linguistic analogy here because it makes this click perfectly for me.

30:30.040 --> 30:35.440
It is like realizing that entanglement is more like grammar than a physical object.

30:35.620 --> 30:36.620
That's a great way to put it.

30:36.820 --> 30:40.640
Imagine you are looking at a string of letters written in the Latin alphabet.

30:41.060 --> 30:44.940
Those letters, the raw data points, are completely fixed on the page.

30:45.060 --> 30:45.280
Okay.

30:45.500 --> 30:45.700
Sure.

30:45.920 --> 30:53.120
But if you apply the grammatical rules of French to that exact string of letters, it might form a perfectly coherent, beautiful sentence.

30:53.660 --> 30:55.960
The parts separate cleanly into distinct words.

30:56.180 --> 30:57.340
They parse correctly.

30:57.340 --> 31:05.240
Right. But if you take the exact same string of letters and you apply the grammatical rules of Spanish, it might turn into absolute inseparable gibberish.

31:05.460 --> 31:08.000
You can't break it apart into valid Spanish words.

31:08.160 --> 31:09.080
The letters didn't change.

31:09.160 --> 31:10.220
The data didn't change.

31:10.320 --> 31:12.340
The physical ink on the page didn't change.

31:13.020 --> 31:17.960
What changed were the background rules of what constitutes a valid local word?

31:17.960 --> 31:25.320
If the underlying language you are using doesn't have the capacity to cleanly separate the parts, the parts appear inextricably entangled.

31:25.820 --> 31:26.340
Wow.

31:26.340 --> 31:36.640
This warns us about the immense danger of projecting our mathematical models onto reality and assuming we are just reading off the fundamental ontology of the universe.

31:36.820 --> 31:38.520
But that raises a massive question.

31:38.520 --> 31:51.260
If our choice of mathematics, using real numbers versus complex numbers, literally dictates whether a physical system is entangled or not, why do physicists universally settle on complex numbers?

31:51.420 --> 31:52.680
Why do they always come back to it?

31:52.740 --> 31:52.880
Yeah.

31:52.940 --> 31:57.080
If complex numbers just erase this specific entanglement, why are they the standard?

31:57.520 --> 32:02.580
Because complex numbers sit at a very specific mathematical Goldilocks point.

32:02.840 --> 32:03.780
Goldilocks point.

32:03.880 --> 32:05.600
Meaning not too hot, not too cold.

32:05.600 --> 32:11.700
Exactly. The text is very clear. Complex numbers aren't some magical ingredient of physical reality.

32:12.180 --> 32:18.400
They're simply an extraordinarily efficient language that packages two critical pieces of information together perfectly.

32:18.520 --> 32:18.880
Which are?

32:19.180 --> 32:24.820
Magnitude, which is the scale or intensity of a probability, and orientation, which is the rotational phase.

32:25.100 --> 32:27.740
Oh, Flyxion calls these packages amplitwists.

32:28.080 --> 32:28.840
Amplitwists, yes.

32:28.840 --> 32:35.620
It sounds a bit like a 1960s dance craze, but the visualization is actually perfect.

32:35.820 --> 32:36.740
It's very descriptive.

32:37.020 --> 32:44.260
When you multiply complex numbers together, you aren't just scaling a value up or down like you do with regular multiplication.

32:44.760 --> 32:46.840
You are simultaneously twisting it.

32:47.000 --> 32:47.900
You're rotating it.

32:47.940 --> 32:51.560
You are rotating its phase in that extra-dimensional space.

32:51.780 --> 32:53.820
You are performing an amplitwist.

32:53.820 --> 32:59.880
Let's bring this back to the dimensional auditing we just did. Remember how real numbers failed when we combined systems?

33:00.100 --> 33:02.040
Right. The 9 versus 10 issue.

33:02.180 --> 33:10.600
The local parts only had 9 dimensions, but the joint whole required 10. The parts came up short. They didn't have enough descriptive power to account for the relationships.

33:10.880 --> 33:15.820
So you might logically ask, if real numbers are too small, why don't we use quaternions?

33:16.040 --> 33:18.600
Right. Quaternions are an even larger number system.

33:18.600 --> 33:24.200
Instead of just one imaginary axis I, quaternions have three different imaginary axes.

33:24.420 --> 33:30.540
So if one imaginary number provides a helpful escape hatch, surely three escape hatches are better, right?

33:30.660 --> 33:32.460
You'd think so. More room to maneuver.

33:32.860 --> 33:40.640
But if you run the exact same tensor product math using quaternions, the mathematical failure happens in the exact opium direction.

33:40.680 --> 33:41.580
So it overshoots.

33:41.580 --> 33:55.340
It completely overshoots. Because of those multiple imaginary axes, the local parts possess so much rotational freedom that when you combine them, they actually generate more parameters than the joint state space can logically hold.

33:55.480 --> 33:56.240
Oh, wow.

33:56.280 --> 33:57.780
The parts over-describe the whole.

33:58.040 --> 34:02.620
So real numbers are too small, they come up short. Quaternions are too big, they overflow the joint space.

34:02.760 --> 34:04.580
And complex numbers are just right.

34:04.720 --> 34:05.800
The Goldilocks point.

34:05.800 --> 34:12.920
Under the complex tensor product, the parameter count of the local parts perfectly matches the parameter count of the joint whole.

34:13.620 --> 34:24.380
They sit at the exact fixed point where the local descriptions are sufficient to map the joint space without generating phantom tenth dimensions and without overflowing the bounds.

34:24.540 --> 34:25.940
It is structurally perfect.

34:26.060 --> 34:26.840
It really is beautiful.

34:26.980 --> 34:28.300
That is incredibly satisfying.

34:28.860 --> 34:32.520
But Flyxion makes a really sharp philosophical point in chapter 12.

34:33.480 --> 34:34.780
The field is not the theory.

34:34.780 --> 34:35.960
An important distinction.

34:36.440 --> 34:40.840
So what does this actually mean for the realness of the imaginary number eye?

34:41.640 --> 34:44.920
Does the universe literally know about imaginary numbers?

34:45.240 --> 34:46.720
Is reality made of math?

34:47.000 --> 34:47.280
No.

34:47.680 --> 34:51.980
And that is a crucial distinction to prevent us from falling into metaphysical traps.

34:52.140 --> 34:54.000
Because it's so easy to do that.

34:54.220 --> 34:56.960
The specific notation of eye is dispensable.

34:56.960 --> 35:05.100
You can absolutely rewrite all of quantum mechanics using only real numbers, provided you use much larger, much clunkier matrices.

35:05.320 --> 35:05.480
Right.

35:05.580 --> 35:06.620
And add extra rules.

35:06.780 --> 35:06.940
